00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.000
higher than the
last time I looked,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.530
which was about a week ago.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.290
So that number is
probably closer to 90

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.830
now actually, so 90
petabyte-- higher than 90.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:40.990
Do I hear 95, 95?

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:41.560
Do I hear 95?

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:44.890
100, 100, 100?

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.780
OK, so I'm way off.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.490
So this is what
I saw from-- this

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.110
was a small sample of the
datasets I was looking at,

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.920
and I probably didn't
extrapolate this

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.170
as accurately as I could have.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.697
But yeah, so a lot.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:59.280
And so that's really
the answer there,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.500
is people are using
a lot of public data.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.600
But it's in BigQuery.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.820
Now, hang on because
the public data we get

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.690
doesn't always come in a CSV,
or it doesn't come pre-loaded

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:10.116
in a BigQuery table, right?

00:05:10.116 --> 00:05:11.990
And that's what I talked
about earlier, where

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.990
there are Googlers that
are building pipelines.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.130
And they're extracting
this data into BigQuery

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.380
because it's an
easy SQL serverless

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.960
implementation that you
could come in and build

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:21.990
on top of right away.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.500
And one of the really exciting
announcements from this week

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.930
is the BigQuery machine
learning introduction

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.240
that allows you to go on
and build machine learning

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.700
applications on top
of your SQL queries

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.150
using declarative statements.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.230
So we have more
than 2,000 tables

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:42.230
in BigQuery for public
datasets with more than 42

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:42.959
billion rows--

00:05:42.959 --> 00:05:44.750
yes, I did count each
of them individually.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.820
Thank you for asking--

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.165
and more than 5,000
distinct users per week.

00:05:49.165 --> 00:05:50.540
And this is a
number we're really

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:54.860
excited about because
if one user is querying

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.680
80 petabytes of
data, that's great,

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.440
but it doesn't necessarily
mean that we're not necessarily

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.290
democratizing that data
for the broader community.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:07.050
But to see that volume number
grow with the number of users

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.050
and with the number of
distinct projects, that's

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.420
really exciting for us
because what that means

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.970
is that we're improving
the access for users.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.700
We're making it easier for
users who may or may not

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.100
have been data users
in the past to come in

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.116
and leverage this data.

00:06:22.116 --> 00:06:25.150
But it's not just BigQuery.

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:28.671
We have public data also in
GCS, so Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:28.671 --> 00:06:30.670
We have more than three
petabytes of data there.

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.800
That includes things
like radar imagery,

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.990
so we have weather radar imagery
going back to 1991, all the way

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.300
until about 15 minutes ago.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.830
And then we have
satellite imagery

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:45.825
as well from the Geostationary
Environmental Satellite 16,

00:06:45.825 --> 00:06:47.530
so GOES-16.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.030
It's a meteorological
satellite that

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.880
orbits over the same point
in the Earth at all times,

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.180
and that's all I know
about satellites.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.080
But it is a high resolution,
both in space and time,

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.590
for imagery, and it
provides us with the ability

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.240
to better understand weather
patterns as they're unfolding.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.600
We have the archive of that
going back to last year

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.300
as well, when it
became available.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.422
So it's a quick overview
of Google BigQuery.

00:07:13.422 --> 00:07:15.880
For those of you that aren't
familiar, I mentioned earlier,

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:17.580
it's a serverless
data warehouse that's

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.780
built to support
big data analysis.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.900
Felipe has got some great
demos on BigQuery coming up,

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.370
but the one thing
I want to hit on

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:28.540
is that you don't have to worry
about developing or managing

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.257
or building the infrastructure.

00:07:30.257 --> 00:07:31.840
It's already built.
All you have to do

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.256
is put your data in BigQuery
and write your SQL statements

00:07:34.256 --> 00:07:35.390
on top of it.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:38.350
And it scales
seamlessly and allows

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:41.000
you to share your
analysis very easily.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.180
So there's a free tier for
storage and for queries.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:46.062
The values are listed up there.

00:07:46.062 --> 00:07:47.770
But what that means
is that you can go in

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.270
and you can start
using the program

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.510
today without incurring
costs, assuming

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:56.371
you're below these values.

00:07:56.371 --> 00:07:57.870
So how can it support
open data use?

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.550
We have a few very
general profiles up here.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.466
So the first one is businesses.

00:08:02.466 --> 00:08:03.840
I've talked to
several businesses

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.140
who have told us that they
exist because the public data is

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.361
in GCP.

00:08:08.361 --> 00:08:10.110
And what it allows
them to do is it allows

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:11.550
them to focus on their idea.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.490
They don't have to focus
on building data access

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.090
mechanisms.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.700
They don't have to focus on
maintaining these data access

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:18.990
mechanisms or storing them.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.240
They can focus on
building their product

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.465
and delivering their
product to their users.

00:08:23.465 --> 00:08:25.590
And they can do so on top
of these public datasets,

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.780
either by joining with their
proprietary data, which

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.000
we think is a really
valuable way to use it,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.870
or by building their
product on top of the data

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.870
to deliver new
insights to users.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.179
For researchers, you can
join multiple public datasets

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:41.120
together to do an
analysis in ways that

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.287
have never been done before.

00:08:42.287 --> 00:08:43.953
One of the things
that really excites me

00:08:43.953 --> 00:08:45.870
is the ability to
bring data together

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.496
that has previously
lived in silos that

00:08:47.496 --> 00:08:49.328
aren't even on the same
farm, if we're going

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:50.970
to stick with the silo analogy.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.030
So you're looking at data that
has just never been brought

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.910
together before, or has
not been brought together

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.550
in such an easy-to-use
format before, that

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:02.460
can be joined together to
find some new insights.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.010
And then for data providers,
you can provide fast and simple

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.750
access to your
data without having

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.220
to scale your on-prem services.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.670
So your storage scales
to a million users

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:15.450
or to one user for
the same effort.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.076
So without further
introduction, Felipe.

00:09:18.076 --> 00:09:19.200
FELIPE HOFFA: Hello, hello.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.070
Can you hear me?

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:21.520
Thank you.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:22.870
Thank you, Shane.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.440
So I'm Felipe Hoffa,
and Shane gave me

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.920
this time to talk about
issues that matter to me.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:32.850
Last year, I showed space
versus tabs on code,

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.730
but today I want to
speak about a real issue.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.960
People are fighting on
the internet about this.

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:40.470
Where do we put our commas?

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.270
And I'm talking about SQL.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.680
Who knows SQL here?

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:45.450
Excellent.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.950
So SQL is awesome.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.520
It's a language
invented in 1978.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:51.610
We still use it.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:53.400
We still love it.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.020
You might have learned SQL
with a query like this.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:56.520
SELECT name.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:57.240
FROM employees.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.250
WHERE-- they come
from some state.

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.260
Then you will want to
get more columns there,

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.380
and then you add a
third column, and then

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.120
suddenly, things don't work.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.490
Why?

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:11.700
Because I have this hanging
comma here, and this

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:15.330
is because language creators
decided that you could not

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:16.470
put a comma there.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.600
Every other modern language
will allow a comma,

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.610
but SQL will not.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:23.430
So now, people are in two camps.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:25.680
Some people put their
comments at the end.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.310
Some people put their
commas at the start.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:32.750
Who puts their
commas at the end?

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:35.520
Who puts their
commas at the start?

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:41.040
Two, three, four-- that's super
raggedly, but that's what I do,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:41.790
too.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.290
It's much more
efficient for me if I

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.680
put my commas at the start,
given the language limitation.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.420
So now the question
is, can I use

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.060
data to prove to
everyone else that this

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.570
is what you should do?

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:55.360
So trailing commas,
leading commas.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:56.170
We are Google.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.380
We have Google Cloud.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:10:59.370
We have BigQuery.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:04.680
We have data that everyone
can see, share, and analyze.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:08.340
I will look at Github just
to count the number of events

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:11.580
that we had on Github last year.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:12.640
Give me three seconds.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:17.220
Five, four-- 300 million events.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.270
I can go further.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.850
I can count, for example,
the number of distinct users.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.140
And I can look at where
are they coming from?

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:25.440
What are the most
important countries here?

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:31.020
The most developers come from
null country, then the US, then

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.850
China, India.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.440
But that's not a fair ranking.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.480
I should do it per capita.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.500
So who has more
programmers per capita?

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:43.410
It turns out it's
Iceland, Sweden, Norway--

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.560
cold countries.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:50.796
Now, is it true that it's
cold countries or not?

00:11:50.796 --> 00:11:51.870
I also have NOAA data.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:52.780
I have weather data.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:55.530
So if I take, on one hand,
my programmers per capita,

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:58.040
I take the average
weather of each country,

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.870
I can get to a chart like this
that shows that, yeah, there

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:02.730
is a correlation.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.910
People in cold countries
have a higher concentration

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:06.750
of programmers.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.840
Warmer countries-- lower
concentration of programmers.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:14.700
Except Singapore there,
because they have very good AC,

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.840
so programmers
prefer to say inside.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.200
Real query.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:22.270
So let's go back to this issue.

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:25.800
Let's use-- also we have
Github calls inside the query.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.410
And I am able to analyze,
are people using more commas

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.570
at the end or at the beginning?

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:31.660
This is my query.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:34.560
And the results show
that way more people

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.564
use a comma at the end.

00:12:37.564 --> 00:12:41.770
But this is not really
what I want to know.

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:43.510
I don't want to know
what's more popular.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:46.656
I want to know what projects
are more successful.

00:12:46.656 --> 00:12:49.660
And how do you measure success?

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:50.730
A larger number of stars?

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.180
Stars last year?

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:54.490
The number of
contributors, activity?

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:57.310
So I can write a bigger query
that looks for all of these,

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:59.670
and turns out that
these are my results.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.830
And projects that allow
people to use leading commas

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:06.200
are double as successful
as the other projects.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:08.810
So yes, we win.

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:09.950
Excellent.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:12.540
And yes, BigQuery connects
to all these tools,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:16.800
and you will be able to
use open data to share.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.140
Even if these
fights do not stop,

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:24.060
at least you can embrace
data when going through them.

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.280
I want to show you a couple
of more features that are new,

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:30.780
and I'm super
excited about them.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:34.040
Have you heard
about Data Studio?

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.890
Yes, so with Data
Studio now, I'm

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:38.810
able to take my
BigQuery analysis,

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:41.420
and I can create
interactive visualizations.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:44.390
I recently created this
one for Stack Overflow,

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.150
and I can embed
my visualizations

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:50.000
in the middle of my blog post.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.735
I can make them interactive.

00:13:51.735 --> 00:13:54.090
These are the trends
on Stack Overflow.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.570
Let's look, for
example, Angular.js, one

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.910
of the top tags on this site.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:03.070
Angular.js is going up or down?

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.440
Down.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.110
Why is Angular.js going down?

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:12.432
Because they replaced it with
a new tag that is Angular.

00:14:12.432 --> 00:14:15.510
So whatever technology
you are interested in,

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:19.920
people can come, play
here, and use public data.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:23.340
On the satellite side,
this is outside BigQuery.

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:26.190
My friend [? Lac, ?] who is
a great friend of everyone

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.510
that knows about machine
learning and weather

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:31.740
forecasting.

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.770
He created this great sample
looking at hurricanes.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.680
We have in BigQuery at
least of all the hurricanes,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.040
you can see what's happening.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:40.710
So we don't know when
it's the next one,

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.350
but we know all
the previous ones.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.230
You can use it to
get their data,

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:50.460
and then you can find the
files, the satellite images,

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:53.490
that correspond to those
hurricanes that [INAUDIBLE],,

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.660
and finally can produce--

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:59.330
get individual images,
create animations,

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:02.380
and look at what is happening
all around the world

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.660
with just some
few lines of code.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:07.800
That's the power of having
the public datasets available.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.530
And the last thing I want
to show for everyone that

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:13.830
is a fan of BigQuery and
has seen my Wikipedia

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.290
demo, for example,
in this demo, I'm

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:20.720
going to process 377
gigabytes of data

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:23.440
that I'm going through
billions of page views

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:26.130
that Wikipedia has in a month.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:27.650
When I run this
query, I just want

00:15:27.650 --> 00:15:31.210
the sum of views for Google.

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:35.850
This query is processing
377 gigabytes, which

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:42.390
is an awesome demo, but since
you have one free terabyte

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.790
every month, you
would be able only

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:46.590
to run three of these queries.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:51.290
So good as a demo, but not
so good for public data.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:54.420
And the best news we
got, for me, this--

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:57.150
yesterday, is that now
we can cluster tables.

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:01.750
We can run-- we can
set data together.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:07.380
So now, when I run my query
over this new table, same query,

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.270
same amount of data, same--

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:15.330
we think it will
process 375 gigabytes.

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:19.990
Instead, it's only went
over 18 gigabytes of data.

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:23.010
So we are taking our poly
datasets program now.

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:25.250
We are going to
cluster all tables

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:26.850
and make it way more effective.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:29.500
So yes, [INAUDIBLE],,
I'm looking at you.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:32.070
[INAUDIBLE] we're going
to make these tables

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:37.330
all time partitioned and
clustered by the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:43.690
And with that, let me welcome
Shane back to take it over.

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:45.136
Thank you.

00:16:45.136 --> 00:16:47.540
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:47.934 --> 00:16:49.100
SHANE GLASS: Thanks, Felipe.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.060
In the interest of
full disclosure,

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.820
I didn't start
using leading commas

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:57.200
until I saw Felipe's slides
and realized how wrong I was.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:59.639
Now, I did see in
Felipe's demo, however,

00:16:59.639 --> 00:17:02.180
that there are people who are
using both leading and trailing

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:06.200
commas, and those people just
want to watch the world burn.

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.033
I'm terrified of you.

00:17:08.033 --> 00:17:08.699
You're monsters.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:14.589
So next up, we have Ed
Kearns here from NOAA.

00:17:14.589 --> 00:17:16.950
Ed is NOAA's chief data officer.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:19.284
He's the first chief
data officer of NOAA,

00:17:19.284 --> 00:17:20.700
and he's been a
great collaborator

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:22.050
of the public dataset program.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:25.509
And I'm looking forward
to hearing from him.

00:17:25.509 --> 00:17:27.974
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:30.239 --> 00:17:31.530
ED KEARNS: Thank you very much.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.170
So you wonder why there's a
government employee up here

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.675
talking at Google, but
we have this partnership

00:17:36.675 --> 00:17:37.800
that I'm going to describe.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.800
And you've seen the results
of this partnership.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:41.140
It's very, very powerful.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:45.240
So one of the things that I do
as NOAA's chief data officer,

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:50.880
I'm trying to find the maximum
exploitation of the taxpayer's

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:54.090
dollar that you've paid for
these expensive satellites.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:57.330
You've paid for a lot
of collection systems

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:00.720
that bring data from the
environment into NOAA,

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:03.540
and we use that for our mission.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.750
But we also realize there's a
secondary use for this data.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:08.929
So we're using it for
managing fisheries.

00:18:08.929 --> 00:18:10.470
We're using it for
weather forecasts.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:12.690
We're using it for
managing our ecosystems

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:14.010
along the coastlines.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:16.770
But we understand that you, as
maybe a small business owner,

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.177
can also use this public
open data for other purposes.

00:18:19.177 --> 00:18:20.760
And we understand
there's value there,

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:23.920
and we want to
maximize that value.

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:27.750
And so, like I said,
we have our mission,

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:34.614
but the secondary use is,
how can we maximize this?

00:18:34.614 --> 00:18:36.030
This started about
four years ago.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.400
We realized, well, we
don't have the resources

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:41.100
within the federal government
to fully exploit the data.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:43.020
But hey, Google does.

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:45.900
And can we find a way
that we can work together

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.630
in a partnership
to actually bring

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.930
that value forward and
democratize the data,

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:52.650
as Shane was mentioning?

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.450
So let me first explain
how big NOAA is.

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:58.120
So we're a fairly
big organization.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:59.910
We have a very
wide scope of what

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.140
we do for the American public.

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:03.420
We have about 12,000 people.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.720
Of those 12,000 people,
we've got 7,000 scientists.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:09.170
And all those scientists
work with data every day.

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:10.740
They are oceanographers,
like I am.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.130
They're meteorologists.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:13.050
They're biologists.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:14.520
They're fisheries experts.

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:18.000
Every single one of them
is also a data scientist

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:22.560
in their realm of expertise,
because data is the heartblood

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:23.400
of our organization.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:25.733
Nothing happens in NOAA--
none of these products get out

00:19:25.733 --> 00:19:27.090
for the American people--

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:30.386
without data flowing
through them.

00:19:30.386 --> 00:19:31.760
I also have an
enormous challenge

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:33.800
if I want to share this data.

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:37.820
So at any given day, we've
got about 200 petabytes

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:41.090
of data residing across
NOAA's federal systems.

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:43.310
These are not in
the cloud, largely.

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:47.120
These are within-- behind
firewalls on federal systems

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:48.580
across the United States.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:52.460
We got about 70,000 datasets,
so that's an enormous number

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.760
to try to understand.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:57.560
If you just go out on
the regular internet,

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:00.120
and you try to find
all these datasets,

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:02.450
and they are almost--
probably 95% of these

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:05.720
are publicly available
today in an open fashion

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:07.800
if you can find where they are.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.480
And so that's the challenge.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:12.350
We've got 1,200
registered web domains.

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:15.710
We got 240,000
individual websites

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:17.550
that are out there
that all have data.

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:20.300
So it's very hard for you
all to find and consume

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:21.900
the data that we have.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:24.687
But we try to find
different ways of doing it.

00:20:24.687 --> 00:20:26.270
And we're trying to
embrace the cloud,

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:28.340
trying to bring new
technologies in.

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:30.710
Last year, we did use
the cloud in order

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:32.960
to try to make
sure everybody that

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:37.290
needed hurricane data, hurricane
forecast data, before Hurricane

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.400
Irma hit Florida, that
they had access to it.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:41.900
And last year,
yes, indeed, we're

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:43.421
getting a billion hits a day.

00:20:43.421 --> 00:20:45.170
And because we're using
cloud technologies

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:46.880
in front of our
federal services,

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.700
we're able to do that,
so a great success story.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:53.720
But we are also usually the
number two or number three

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:56.690
website in all of the
United States government

00:20:56.690 --> 00:21:01.500
because so many people
need our data every day.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:03.960
So in order to use this
data, there's two challenges.

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:05.000
One is technical.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:06.380
How do you get the data?

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:09.050
And the example that
Felipe just showed

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:11.120
that [? Lac ?] had
put together really

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:14.420
shows how working with
Google has simplified this,

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:16.520
because before
that, if you wanted

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:18.999
to work with our satellite data
and work with our hurricane

00:21:18.999 --> 00:21:21.290
data, you had to download
all of these different things

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:24.670
back to your own
local computer site,

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:28.410
or wherever it might be, and
then try to do things with it.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:30.990
What this example showed is
you can do everything in place.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:32.990
So some of our datasets
or climate models

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:34.770
are about a petabyte in size.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.470
It's really hard even
to move that data

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:39.640
within our own organization,
much less get it out to you

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:41.090
so you can use it.

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:43.130
So these are some of the
technical challenges.

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:48.260
But the bigger challenge
that we're trying to fix

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:50.986
is the understanding,
to try to figure out

00:21:50.986 --> 00:21:52.610
what these data mean,
and how to convey

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.120
that understanding to you.

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:55.700
So I've got 7,000
scientists that

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:57.560
know exactly what
those data mean,

00:21:57.560 --> 00:21:59.894
but how do they interact with
you to convey the meaning?

00:21:59.894 --> 00:22:02.226
Because like I said, we've
got the data parked out there

00:22:02.226 --> 00:22:02.780
already.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:05.090
We've had the data parked
out there for many years,

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:07.610
and it's not getting picked
up and used in the kind of way

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:09.740
that it is being used when
we're seeing we put it

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:10.880
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:14.320
And that's what we're
trying to learn more about.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:16.209
So what we did four
years ago is we

00:22:16.209 --> 00:22:18.000
signed what's called
a Cooperative Research

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.630
and Development
Agreement, or a CRADA.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:22.950
It's a government
term, but it's a way

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:26.370
of actually having an experiment
going on with industry where

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:29.670
there's no money exchanged,
but where we have agreed

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:31.284
to work on a common problem.

00:22:31.284 --> 00:22:33.450
And we've been doing this
for four years with Google

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:33.950
right now.

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:36.270
So this comes at no
cost to the taxpayer.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:38.580
The trick here is that
these data are open.

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:39.930
Data that remain open.

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:42.120
Google is not selling the data.

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:43.991
They're selling
services around the data

00:22:43.991 --> 00:22:45.490
so they can recoup
their investment,

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:48.840
but they are hosting the
taxpayers' data from NOAA

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.160
for free.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:53.437
And so we understand
how that can scale,

00:22:53.437 --> 00:22:55.770
and you just saw examples of
this, of how the clouds can

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:57.300
scale and the open
data can scale,

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:58.950
the availability can scale.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:00.780
But to really get
over the hurdle,

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.610
we're focused on the
expertise, because I

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.190
can park a lot of this
complicated data out there

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.500
and you still won't know
exactly how to use it.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:10.680
So we have to solve this.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:12.700
So you heard about
some of the examples

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:15.740
with some of the
successes already.

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.240
The sweet spot that we found,
instead of just parking

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:20.965
the data out there, if we're
loading the data into the tools

00:23:20.965 --> 00:23:23.340
that people are already using--
and BigQuery is a perfect

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:24.600
example of this--

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:27.870
by loading NOAA's weather
data into BigQuery,

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.350
people can come and use that
data, join on that data,

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:32.880
consume that data.

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:35.350
They don't have to break down
a complicated scientific data

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:35.850
format.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.980
They don't have to try to
understand what all these mean.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.230
They understand, hey, it's
temperature, precipitation,

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:41.850
it's cloudiness, it's wind.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:43.170
They understand what it is.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.189
It's already loaded into
tools they're using.

00:23:45.189 --> 00:23:46.980
They can immediately
consume it and use it.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:50.310
That's a very exciting
paradigm, and so we're trying

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:51.510
to see how we leverage this.

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:54.220
So we're comparing an A/B test--

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:56.970
how is this working on Google
versus how it's working

00:23:56.970 --> 00:23:59.130
from our own federal servers?

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:00.810
And we're seeing
at least 10 times--

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:03.619
probably between 10 and
100 times more consumption

00:24:03.619 --> 00:24:05.910
of these weather data coming
out of BigQuery than we're

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:08.850
seeing from our own
internal services right now.

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.890
And this came--
when we first did--

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:15.180
when we first started this
about a year ago now, I guess,

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:17.270
when we first got
this off the ground,

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:20.040
one of the interesting things
is we didn't advertise it.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:23.520
NOAA didn't advertise the
availability of these weather

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:24.990
data in BigQuery yet.

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:27.030
Google did not advertise it yet.

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:29.730
They were still thinking about
how they were going to proceed.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.400
But while we're talking about
what we're going to do next,

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:35.099
users of BigQuery came in
and found the data anyways,

00:24:35.099 --> 00:24:36.390
and they started to consume it.

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:37.920
It required no advertising.

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:40.000
We didn't have to put
out any press release

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:41.400
that these data were available.

00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:43.108
People were just able
to find and use it,

00:24:43.108 --> 00:24:44.220
and that's a great story.

00:24:46.810 --> 00:24:50.350
Being able to combine
datasets-- and the example

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.579
that Felipe showed
with the hurricanes

00:24:52.579 --> 00:24:54.370
that [? Lac ?] put
together, because again,

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:56.620
just a great example
of bringing multiple

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:59.870
datasets together that
otherwise may be hard to do,

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:02.620
but when they're all in the
same place on the same cloud,

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:06.632
it becomes really trivial.

00:25:06.632 --> 00:25:08.340
And so where are we
going with this, too?

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:12.240
So we're trying to
find-- there's increasing

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.039
levels of usage for our data.

00:25:14.039 --> 00:25:16.080
We're trying to make sure
you, the data consumer,

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:17.430
can get the data more quickly.

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:19.260
We have our archive system.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.210
We have 30 petabytes
in our archive.

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.160
It is still a
tape-based archive.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:24.870
The archive is
primarily preservation,

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:26.460
but we make all those data open.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:29.340
It is very hard to get 30
petabytes out of a tape

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:31.240
archive, as you all know.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:33.260
However, if those data
are sitting on the cloud,

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:34.860
it's fairly trivial.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:36.360
And so we're looking
at ways that we

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.734
can continue to raise that level
of service and do so in a way

00:25:39.734 --> 00:25:41.150
that the taxpayer
is not burdened.

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:44.000
So we're trying to leverage
that value that's in the data,

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:48.090
the value to Google's
users, Google's customers,

00:25:48.090 --> 00:25:50.440
trying to leverage
that value to enable

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:54.700
this new way of providing
access to federal data.

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:57.197
And so we've been doing
this for four years--

00:25:57.197 --> 00:25:58.530
well, a little over three years.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.113
It will be four years
when we're done.

00:26:00.113 --> 00:26:03.135
But we're really trying to say,
how can we do this sustainably?

00:26:03.135 --> 00:26:05.010
So we're doing this
experimentally right now.

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:06.550
We're excited about the results.

00:26:06.550 --> 00:26:09.820
But how do we take
this to the next level?

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:12.000
And I really need to
hear from you all.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:13.680
The federal government
needs to hear,

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.810
is this something that you like?

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:18.690
Do you like to get
NOAA's federal data

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:20.130
on the Google Cloud?

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:22.510
If you find this valuable,
we need to hear about it.

00:26:22.510 --> 00:26:23.942
We need to be able to--

00:26:23.942 --> 00:26:26.400
as we're going to our next
level of discussions with Google

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:28.620
about how can this work,
how can this partnership

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:32.280
work sustainably, going on for
many years into the future?

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:34.967
Because NOAA's bringing
the expertise to the table.

00:26:34.967 --> 00:26:37.050
So that's what-- we're not
just bringing the data,

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:38.700
we're bringing the expertise,
and Google's bringing

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.020
their infrastructure.

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:43.260
How do we maintain that
business relationship?

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:47.659
We found, and Google's
found it very valuable

00:26:47.659 --> 00:26:49.950
to be able to have those
discussions with NOAA experts,

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:53.200
to literally do the
deep dive on ocean data,

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.520
to actually have those
one-on-one discussions

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:57.478
and be able to turn that
around and figure out,

00:26:57.478 --> 00:26:59.460
how do those products
fit into the Google tools

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.980
and meet the demands
of their users?

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:05.530
So we're looking
for that right now.

00:27:05.530 --> 00:27:10.020
There's also a federal
data strategy effort

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:11.820
that has just begun, as well.

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:15.522
And if you go to
strategy.data.gov,

00:27:15.522 --> 00:27:17.400
we're in an open
public comment period

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:21.720
right now, where the federal
government is asking the data

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:24.820
users across the
country, what do you

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.070
want to see with federal data?

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.200
Do you like things like
this kind of partnership

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.420
with industry that
allow federal data to be

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:32.610
available on the cloud?

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:36.750
Or would you rather pick it
up from a federal service?

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:39.570
These are real questions that
we're dealing with right now.

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:41.550
The timing is fantastic,
but honestly, this

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:43.895
is a time that we need
to hear your voice.

00:27:43.895 --> 00:27:45.270
If you go to
BigQuery, and you've

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:47.370
been experimenting with
these public datasets,

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:50.210
if this resonates with you,
there's a time right now

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:53.700
to be able to speak up
and be heard and have

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:58.170
this kind of partnership,
this cloud-based data access,

00:27:58.170 --> 00:28:01.860
baked into how the federal
data strategy is going

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:03.310
to be rolling into the future.

00:28:03.310 --> 00:28:05.740
And timing is
everything in life.

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:09.480
And so this CRADA came
along at the perfect time.

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:13.140
The results have come along,
again, just in the nick of time

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:15.570
so that these things can be
integrated into the wider

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:16.650
strategy.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:19.470
And yeah, please, please--

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:21.750
I welcome, if you
want to drop me

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:24.330
an email at this
email address, or you

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:26.360
want to go and
turn your comments

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:29.220
in at strategy.data.gov,
I encourage

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:33.026
you to do so and
welcome your feedback.

00:28:33.026 --> 00:28:35.380
And I think I'm turning it
back over to Shane, now.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:36.147
Thank you.

00:28:36.147 --> 00:28:38.632
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:42.081 --> 00:28:43.330
SHANE GLASS: Yeah, thanks, Ed.

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:47.059
And I think Ed touched
on something big,

00:28:47.059 --> 00:28:49.100
and there's the example
of it here on this slide.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:52.090
So in case you haven't heard
enough about [? Lac's ?] demo,

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:53.890
we've got a little
bit more here for you.

00:28:53.890 --> 00:28:55.990
But I think that one
of the things that's--

00:28:55.990 --> 00:28:59.389
I think this is a true
big data application.

00:28:59.389 --> 00:29:01.180
And big data is one of
those buzzwords that

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:03.890
is used to mean everything,
and as a result,

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:06.670
it really means nothing
because it's just used broadly,

00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:08.930
it doesn't really have
a meaning these days.

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:11.690
But I think this is really
a big data application.

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:14.170
Now, data is the new oil.

00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:15.334
We've all heard that.

00:29:15.334 --> 00:29:17.500
This is not an original
thought that Shane just had.

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:19.570
But I've kind of thought
about this phrase a lot.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:21.320
I've kind of gone
back and forth on it,

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:24.950
and I think that my opinion
on that phrase is sort of.

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:26.740
Data's sort of the new oil.

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:28.660
And I think that
the analogy is--

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:32.290
it's imperfect, but I think
it has some value in that data

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:35.140
in its rawest form has value.

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.780
So having raw oil, just like
having raw data, yes, that

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:39.970
has some value.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:43.510
But being able to process
that data, to convert it

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:45.940
into insights, into
information, just

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:49.870
like being able to process oil
into gasoline, into plastics,

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:52.090
that is really where
the valuable application

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:53.110
of this product is.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:55.910
And that's what we're trying
to unlock for our users.

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:59.410
So what you see on the left is
a sample of the BigQuery table

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:01.870
that [? Lac ?] used
for his demonstration

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:05.450
on visualizing the imagery
from Hurricane Maria.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:07.770
And so what that is,
is it's called IBTrACS.

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:09.310
There's a very
long acronym there,

00:30:09.310 --> 00:30:11.309
but it's essentially the
international community

00:30:11.309 --> 00:30:13.660
coming together and
saying, we agree

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:16.240
that at this place and
time, this hurricane

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:17.730
was at this place.

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:21.130
It is a shockingly difficult
thing to come to agreement on.

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:24.250
But what we've found is that,
that doesn't always line up.

00:30:24.250 --> 00:30:27.160
It's not always associated
with the satellite imagery

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:29.090
that you see on the right there.

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:32.000
And what we found was that those
are in such different places,

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:33.700
they're in such
disparate locations,

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:35.770
that they're very
rarely combined.

00:30:35.770 --> 00:30:38.880
Moreover, while the data
on the left are in CSV,

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:43.040
or available in CSV, the data
on the right are in netCDF4.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:45.160
Is anybody familiar
with the netCDF4 file

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:48.269
format that is not employed
by the federal government?

00:30:48.269 --> 00:30:49.810
Yeah, so we have a
handful of people,

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:51.634
which is a handful more--

00:30:51.634 --> 00:30:53.800
we have a handful of people,
which is a handful more

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:55.060
than I expected.

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:58.450
So netCDF4 is great for
the scientific community

00:30:58.450 --> 00:30:59.190
because it does--

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.030
it compresses very well, and
it does a lot of other things

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:01.840
very well.

00:31:01.840 --> 00:31:04.300
But it's not-- but it's
very, very specific

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:05.770
to the scientific community.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:08.152
The general data science
community, the data scientists

00:31:08.152 --> 00:31:10.360
out there, as opposed to
the environmental scientists

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:13.030
out there, aren't
used to using this.

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:15.170
So that's a barrier to entry.

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:16.240
You have to go out.

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:17.500
You have to find these data.

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:19.690
You have to find the
Python library for it.

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:22.107
You have to find out how to
use the Python library for it.

00:31:22.107 --> 00:31:24.606
You have to find out where to
find out how to use the Python

00:31:24.606 --> 00:31:25.527
library for it.

00:31:25.527 --> 00:31:26.360
You have to go back.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.700
You have to write
your Python for it.

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:29.050
You have to go eat dinner
because at this point,

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:30.310
it's already been all day.

00:31:30.310 --> 00:31:33.530
And then maybe you
can get an analysis

00:31:33.530 --> 00:31:36.240
unless you're working
for the government.

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:39.600
But what we can do here is we
can lower that barrier to entry

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:42.600
by putting the data from
IBTrACS in BigQuery.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.250
It's now a SQL
query away, and we

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:48.990
can subsample by, in this
case, by hurricane name.

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:51.010
And we can take that
very easily on the right,

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:54.450
and we can associate that
with the same center points

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:57.950
from the satellite imagery
that has the hurricane.

00:31:57.950 --> 00:32:00.750
And now you can go from
data to insights faster.

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:02.880
You can go from oil
to gasoline faster.

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:06.360
You can get the value out of
your data as soon as you can,

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:09.390
as opposed to having to wait
and go through this process.

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:11.070
We've worked very
hard to eliminate

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:13.850
all those barriers that entry.

00:32:13.850 --> 00:32:17.080
Now, this slide was made before
the announcement was made.

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:20.000
However, BigQuery launched
their GeoViz capability,

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.424
their geovisualization
capability, this week.

00:32:22.424 --> 00:32:23.715
And that takes us even further.

00:32:26.890 --> 00:32:30.384
So we've talked a lot about
what we've done in the past.

00:32:30.384 --> 00:32:32.300
You've heard a lot of
our metrics in the past.

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:34.800
You've heard Ed talk about the
great collaboration we've had

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:37.040
so far, and you've heard
Felipe talk about some

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:39.498
of the things that can be done
with the datasets we already

00:32:39.498 --> 00:32:40.660
have.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:44.392
And we talked a little bit
about what we want to do.

00:32:44.392 --> 00:32:46.600
We talked about how we're
working with NOAA right now

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:48.475
to figure out, what's
a sustainable long-term

00:32:48.475 --> 00:32:51.420
partnership beyond the end
of our agreement next year?

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:53.770
And so let's talk a little
about what we're going to do.

00:32:53.770 --> 00:32:55.570
So this is a look at
our mission and vision

00:32:55.570 --> 00:32:58.940
statement for the program
going into next year.

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:01.120
So our mission is to
maximize the availability

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:04.390
and accessibility of
flagship public datasets

00:33:04.390 --> 00:33:06.250
in Google Cloud.

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:11.360
We're looking at things
like large, large scale

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:14.444
datasets that are putting
such a burden on our providers

00:33:14.444 --> 00:33:16.610
that they have to limit the
access available to them

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:20.190
now in order to protect their
systems from being brought down

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:21.910
and overwhelmed.

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:25.110
And our vision here is to
enable the development,

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:28.560
and to leverage collaborations,
with key public dataset

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:32.220
providers like NOAA built
around on high impact verticals.

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:35.880
And so the goal here is to
take these weather datasets

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.130
and not just make them
available to the meteorologists

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:40.410
of the world, but to
make them available to

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:42.074
the entire community for use.

00:33:42.074 --> 00:33:43.990
And let's see what cool
stuff comes out of it.

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:45.480
Let's see what people
are able to learn.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:47.105
Let's see what people
are able to build

00:33:47.105 --> 00:33:48.580
for themselves going forward.

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:56.060
So looking at the way ahead,
we're really, really excited

00:33:56.060 --> 00:33:57.710
about the launch
of BigQuery on ML.

00:33:57.710 --> 00:34:01.580
It allows you to build
regression models and cluster

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:04.260
analyses, among other things,
on top of your datasets.

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:07.430
And we think that this helps
you create the only machine

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:12.980
learning ready public dataset
program available today.

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:16.370
All you have to
do to use machine

00:34:16.370 --> 00:34:19.666
learning on top of BigQuery
public datasets today

00:34:19.666 --> 00:34:20.922
is go write SQL.

00:34:20.922 --> 00:34:23.130
You don't have to-- yes,
you have to clean your data.

00:34:23.130 --> 00:34:26.230
And unfortunately, no,
I know very few people

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:27.790
who like to clean data.

00:34:27.790 --> 00:34:30.550
But you don't have to find it.

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:31.540
You don't discover it.

00:34:31.540 --> 00:34:32.500
You don't have to move it.

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:33.958
You don't have to
learn TensorFlow.

00:34:33.958 --> 00:34:36.429
TensorFlow's great, but you
don't have to learn TensorFlow.

00:34:36.429 --> 00:34:38.350
You can just write
your analysis,

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:40.590
and you can just get started.

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:42.630
The BigQuery
geovisualization capability,

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:44.699
we're really excited
about that in combination

00:34:44.699 --> 00:34:46.407
with the second point
of the slide, which

00:34:46.407 --> 00:34:48.010
is utility datasets.

00:34:48.010 --> 00:34:49.770
So what we're focused
on going forward

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:53.690
is enabling our partners, like
NOAA, to onboard the datasets

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.074
into our program.

00:34:55.074 --> 00:34:56.699
Now, does that mean
that Google's never

00:34:56.699 --> 00:34:58.700
going to onboard and
curate datasets again?

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:00.600
No, what it means
is that we're going

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:02.730
to continue our
focus on datasets

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:05.200
that are more broadly
used across all domains.

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:07.170
So we have a group
of datasets that we

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:08.629
call the utility datasets.

00:35:08.629 --> 00:35:10.170
They're just things
that we generally

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:14.400
find useful for all users or
for large swaths of users,

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:17.580
regardless of what
their interests are.

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:20.040
One of these datasets
that's available today

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:23.100
is zip code areas, so it gives
you your five digit zip code,

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.650
the town associated with
it, and a few other metrics.

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:28.770
What we've added to it
recently is the polygon

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:31.110
associated with
that zip code area

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:33.270
from the Census
Bureau's website.

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.070
So you could take
today, and you could

00:35:35.070 --> 00:35:38.640
visualize, by zip
code, your data

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:41.954
and take it out to the BigQuery
geovisualization capability,

00:35:41.954 --> 00:35:43.620
and you could visualize
your data today.

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.690
We're hoping to have a
demonstration of that

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:49.960
and a walkthrough query for
that launched very soon.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:51.790
But that capability
is available today,

00:35:51.790 --> 00:35:54.220
as was announced earlier.

00:35:54.220 --> 00:35:56.760
And so our focus is going to
be on providing these datasets

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:59.090
that we just generally
hear across the community

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:02.290
that people need, and allow our
partners and their expertise

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:07.360
to scale out on our platform
to reduce that burden for them.

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:09.760
So I'm really proud to announce
that, in support of this,

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:12.480
we are making a five
petabytes commitment

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:16.640
to public datasets in BigQuery.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:23.810
My personal take is that this
is an unmatched commitment

00:36:23.810 --> 00:36:25.860
in the community right now.

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:28.400
We're making five petabytes
of BigQuery storage

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:31.890
available to host public
datasets going forward.

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:35.840
And that's great, and that's
a ton of structured data.

00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:40.070
But what good does it
do to store data if--

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:43.520
to extract and store data if
it's going to be gone tomorrow?

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:45.920
And we hear this concern a
lot with our public dataset

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:47.900
providers that we
collaborate with.

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:50.660
Why should I invest my
effort, my team's effort,

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:54.396
into uploading the data if
it's going to be gone tomorrow?

00:36:54.396 --> 00:36:55.770
And because of
that, we're really

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:59.500
proud to announce that, in
addition to this five petabytes

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:03.110
commitment, we are making this
commitment for five years.

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:05.470
So this five petabytes
will be available each year

00:37:05.470 --> 00:37:08.310
for the next five years
to host public datasets

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:09.700
in Google BigQuery.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:11.290
We're really excited
about this, and I

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:12.873
think this is going
to enable us to do

00:37:12.873 --> 00:37:14.790
some really great
things in collaboration

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:15.540
with our partners.

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:20.340
So obviously, you wanted
to get started today,

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:21.881
because Felipe, Ed,
and I have gotten

00:37:21.881 --> 00:37:25.519
you so excited about Google's
public dataset program.

00:37:25.519 --> 00:37:26.810
I did not see any eyes rolling.

00:37:26.810 --> 00:37:28.340
That's good.

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:30.020
So if you want to
use public datasets,

00:37:30.020 --> 00:37:32.020
I encourage you to go to
these sites right here.

00:37:32.020 --> 00:37:35.810
That's cloud.google.com
/bigquery/publicdata

00:37:35.810 --> 00:37:39.916
or cloud.google.com/public
datasets.

00:37:39.916 --> 00:37:42.290
On here, you'll find some
descriptions and some overviews

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:45.860
of some of the datasets we have,
as well as some connections out

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:47.026
to sample queries.

00:37:47.026 --> 00:37:48.650
If they're good,
they're written by me.

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:51.290
If not, they're
written by Felipe.

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:54.430
That's obviously the way
that that would work.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:55.990
And so you can connect out.

00:37:55.990 --> 00:37:57.950
And I think that's a
really great way to just

00:37:57.950 --> 00:37:58.950
get started immediately.

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:01.116
You don't have to know
everything about the dataset,

00:38:01.116 --> 00:38:03.710
but you can get an idea for what
the structure of the data are

00:38:03.710 --> 00:38:06.190
and for how to use them and
then kind of build from there.

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:08.930
So you don't have to
build from scratch.

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:10.500
Do you have public data?

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:12.540
I want to be friends with you.

00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:14.666
If you have public data,
you can get started today.

00:38:14.666 --> 00:38:16.040
You can go to
BigQuery or you can

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:17.700
go to Google Cloud
Storage and leverage

00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:21.190
the free tier of storage that
we talked about earlier today.

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:24.780
So if you're under 10
gigabytes a month for BigQuery,

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:25.710
you can go in today.

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:27.668
You can store that, and
you can make it public,

00:38:27.668 --> 00:38:29.100
and you can share
it with anybody.

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:30.960
And because of the
way BigQuery bills,

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:33.880
it's the person running the
query that is billed for that,

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:35.980
so you aren't billed for that.

00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:37.860
Same thing with
Google Cloud Storage.

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:39.300
I just obviously
have a note there

00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:40.770
that before you share
it with other people,

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:42.145
unless you want
to pay the costs,

00:38:42.145 --> 00:38:44.430
I would put it on a requester
pays mechanism for that.

00:38:44.430 --> 00:38:46.504
So I think I'm safe on that one.

00:38:46.504 --> 00:38:48.420
So maybe you're over the
free tier of storage.

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:51.270
Maybe you have a ton of public
data, and that's even better.

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:53.520
I want to be even
better friends with you.

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:58.770
Feel free to reach out to us
at gcp-public-data@google.com.

00:38:58.770 --> 00:38:59.690
That goes to myself.

00:38:59.690 --> 00:39:01.860
That goes to a couple of other
of our team members as well,

00:39:01.860 --> 00:39:04.380
and we'd love to get started
working with you on hosting

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:06.930
public data.

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:08.930
So thank you so much,
everybody, for coming out.

00:39:08.930 --> 00:39:11.410
Let's have a round of applause
for Felipe and for Ed.

00:39:11.410 --> 00:39:12.610
And thank you very much.

00:39:12.610 --> 00:39:13.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:16.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

